group_by()


Flags the rows of a data frame as belonging to a group defined by a variable, for use in downstream operations.



star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height)`
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo               1.75
2 Tatooine            1.75

group_by() with summarize()

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height)`
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo               1.75
2 Tatooine            1.75




Makes a summary row for each group.

group_by() + summarize() + n()

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(n = n())



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld     n
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Naboo         2
2 Tatooine      2




Same result as count(star_wars, homeworld) — count() is a shortcut for this exact pattern.

Statefulness
What will this produce?


star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    mutate(height_z = (height - mean(height)) / sd(height)) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height_z))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height_z)`
  &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo                    0
2 Tatooine                 0





Once grouped, a data frame stays grouped until reduced to one-row-per group or it is ungrouped.


can break the pipe after mutate to show it is still grouped

Statefulness
What will this produce?

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    mutate(height_z = (height - mean(height)) / sd(height)) |&gt;
    ungroup() |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height_z))



# A tibble: 1 × 1
  `mean(height_z)`
             &lt;dbl&gt;
1                0

Distinct values: make

zev |&gt;
  filter(make == "Toyota/Subaru") |&gt;
  count(model)



# A tibble: 1 × 2
  model             n
  &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;int&gt;
1 bZ4X/Solterra   444


Discuss: Toyota, Subaru, or its own make?

The only model is the bZ4X/Solterra, one car co-developed by Toyota and sold under both brands. There is no single right answer. For market share by brand (e.g. the Project 2 concentration question), the choice changes the numbers a bit, so it should be stated.

Step 7: Duplicates and keys

Check the keys: a column, or combination of columns, whose values uniquely identify each row.



zev |&gt;
  count(year, quarter, county, fuel_type, make, model) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt; 1)



# A tibble: 0 × 7
# ℹ 7 variables: year &lt;dbl&gt;, quarter &lt;dbl&gt;, county &lt;chr&gt;, fuel_type &lt;chr&gt;,
#   make &lt;chr&gt;, model &lt;chr&gt;, n &lt;int&gt;





No duplicates: these six columns are a key for zev. For exact duplicate rows, compare nrow(df) to nrow(distinct(df)).

